I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the framework’s available diffraction/refinement tools first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, refine them together in GSAS‑II, test coherent residuals in the common multiphase state, and export the requested project and profiles.

The chemistry is WO₃ + Mn₃O₄ fired at 800 °C, so Mn–W–O products plus residual manganese/tungsten oxides are plausible. The scan is a raw 10–100° Cu Kα pattern with unknown zero, specimen displacement, profile, texture, and background, so phase identification has to be coupled to nuisance-parameter refinement rather than judged from peak positions alone.

The observed peak set is already strongly diagnostic: the major reflections at 15.48, 18.44, 23.67, 24.19, 29.98/30.42, 31.30, and the dense high-angle sequence all map one-for-one onto monoclinic wolframite-type MnWO₄ (P2/c). I’m now fitting that atomic model over the full grid; the key remaining checks are whether the residual requires a second crystalline phase and whether texture/strain or reference-coordinate mismatch explains the intensity residuals.

The first whole-pattern fit confirms that MnWO₄ is necessary but not sufficient. A coherent residual series—especially 23.10, 26.56, 28.60, 33.23, 34.12, 41.90, and 56.07°—matches the characteristic monoclinic WO₃ family, so I’m adding WO₃ and will evaluate it jointly with MnWO₄. The initial one-phase Rwp is about 27%, far above counting noise, and is not being treated as an acceptable phase-only explanation.

The two-phase fit lowers Rwp to about 19% and gives roughly 82% MnWO₄ / 18% WO₃, but it exposes a third coherent reflection family at 19.86/20.39, 28.66, 35.09, 46.48, 54.4, and higher angles. Those peaks are not intensity-mismatch artifacts from either fitted phase. I’m screening ordinary Mn-oxide precursors first, then same-framework Mn–W–O analogues if no ordinary reference accounts for that family.

The main phase is clearly wolframite MnWO₄, with monoclinic WO₃ and a weaker residual family consistent with an Mn-substituted A₂WO₆ framework. I’m now validating that third framework against one-/two-element analogues, then I’ll do the final common-state GSAS-II refinement and fresh full-grid replay.

The framework-neighbour search found exactly the expected hierarchy: Y₂WO₆ (one substitution) plus three P2₁2₁2₁ tellurate relatives requiring two substitutions. I’m testing those coordinate variants as additions to the same frozen MnWO₄ + WO₃ project, so any apparent gain is directly comparable.

The direct Y₂WO₆-derived coordinates remain the best P2₁2₁2₁ variant. The full three-phase refinements are converging more slowly because phase-specific size/strain and specimen displacement are now active; early results already show the weak phase has substantially broader crystallites than MnWO₄, which explains why simple stick matching understated it.

